 a mapping from observation histories to actions. The central definitions of FDT (subjunctive dependence, intervention on a logical variable, preservation of logical constraints in counterfactuals) carry over unchanged; what changes is *what gets varied* in the agent's hypotheticals, and this turns out to matter for iterated and multi-agent settings.\n\nThis essay develops P-FDT as a self-contained decision procedure: the formal machinery (§3), the core examples that diagnose CDT and EDT (§4), how P-FDT differs from action-level FDT (§5), and some open questions where the source leaves choices unresolved (§6).\n\n## 2. The Motivating Problem: Broken Counterfactuals\n\nAll three theories are expected utility maximizers. Following the source (drawing on Joyce 1999), an agent executes an action $a$ maximizing\n\n$$\\mathcal{EU}(a) := \\sum_{j} P(a \\hookrightarrow o_j \\mid x)\\,\\mathcal{U}(o_j),$$\n\nwhere $o_j$ ranges over outcomes, $x$ is an observation history, and $P(a \\hookrightarrow o_j \\mid x)$ is the probability that $o_j$ obtains *in the hypothetical where* the agent takes $a$ after observing $x$. Joyce's representation theorem shows that the theories agree on the value of expected utility and disagree only about the \"epistemic perspective\" encoded in the connective $\\hookrightarrow$ — the decision hypothetical.\n\n- **EDT** interprets $\\hookrightarrow$ as conditioning: $P(o_j \\mid x, a)$. This preserves *all* correlations between the action and the world, including merely statistical ones.\n- **CDT** interprets $\\hookrightarrow$ as a Pearl-style causal intervention $\\mathrm{do}(\\text{Act}=a)$ on a causal graph. This preserves only correlations *caused by* the action.\n- **FDT/P-FDT** interprets $\\hookrightarrow$ as an intervention on a *logical* variable — the output of the agent's decision function — preserving all *subjunctively dependent* correlations.\n\nThe source's diagnostic slogan is elegant: EDT respects too many action–environment correlations, CDT respects too few, and FDT gets the balance right. EDT agents \"manage the news\" (refusing to smoke in the smoking lesion problem, paying XOR blackmailers); CDT agents imagine that known-infallible predictors err exactly when it matters (two-boxing in Newcomb's problem, destabilizing in Death in Damascus).\n\n## 3. The Formal Machinery\n\n### 3.1 Subjunctive dependence\n\nTwo physical systems computing the same function have behaviors that **subjunctively depend** on that function: if the function's output were different, both systems' outputs would differ together. The source's image is two calculators on opposite sides of the globe computing $6288 + 1048$: if one outputs $7336$, infer the other does too — not because either causes the other, but because both implement the same mathematical structure. Causal dependence is a special case (a scribe copying a calculator's output is both causally and subjunctively dependent on it); mere statistical correlation is not (two coincidentally pink calculators have no subjunctive dependence between their colors).\n\nA Newcomb predictor contains an accurate representation or simulation of the agent's decision function; the prediction and the action subjunctively depend on that function. A carcinogenic lesion does not reason about the agent's decisions, so the cancer–smoking correlation is purely statistical. This asymmetry is what lets a single rule one-box in Newcomb's problem and smoke in the smoking lesion problem.\n\n### 3.2 From actions to policies\n\nThe source's framework (§5 of the paper) has the agent maintain a world-model $M$ — a probability distribution $P$ augmented with a graph $G$ describing how changing a logical variable affects other variables. For each candidate output, the agent performs surgery on the variable $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{M}, \\underline{x})$ representing the output of her decision procedure on inputs $(M, x)$, using Pearl's $\\mathrm{do}$ operator on this logical-dependency graph. Equation (4) of the source:\n\n$$\\mathrm{FDT}(P, G, x) := \\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}} \\mathbb{E}\\big[\\mathrm{V} \\mid \\mathrm{do}\\big(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x}) = a\\big)\\big].$$\n\nTwo differences from CDT's equation matter. First, the intervention targets a logical fact (the decision function's output), not a physical act. Second — and this is the seed of P-FDT — FDT responds to *observations* not by conditioning but by **changing which node it intervenes on**: observing $x$ means intervening on $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{x})$ rather than $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\underline{y})$ for a different $y$. This is why an FDT agent holding the blackmail letter can coherently imagine a world in which she was never blackmailed: \"agents should entertain the hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have *made different observations*.\"\n\nFootnote 16 upgrades the intervention variable. Define a **policy** as a function $\\pi: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{A}$ from observation histories to actions. The P-FDT agent's model contains a variable $\\mathrm{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ representing *the entire policy output by the P-FDT procedure* — the whole mapping, not its value at one input. The decision rule is:\n\n$$\\textbf{P-FDT}(P, G) := \\arg\\max_{\\pi \\in \\Pi}\\; \\mathbb{E}\\big[\\mathrm{V} \\mid \\mathrm{do}\\big(\\mathrm{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}) = \\pi\\big)\\big],$$\n\nwhere $\\Pi$ is the (finite, in practice) set of policies under consideration, and the agent, having selected the optimal $\\pi^*$, *then* applies $\\pi^*$ to her actual observation $x$ to obtain her action: $a = \\pi^*(x)$.\n\nNote the structural shift: the expectation in the P-FDT rule is taken *before* conditioning on or intervening on the observation. The agent evaluates each candidate policy by asking what the world would look like if her decision procedure output that whole mapping, and how it would play out *across all the observations she might receive*. This is closely related to the move in Wei Dai's updateless decision theory (which the source's footnote on \"lack of updating\" identifies as an early variant of functional decision theory): the observation is treated as something the *policy* responds to, not as something to be conditioned on before choosing.\n\n### 3.3 What the counterfactuals preserve\n\nAs in action-level FDT, the $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{pfdt} = \\pi)$ operation updates everything *subjunctively downstream* of the policy variable: the agent's action at each observation point (insofar as her body implements the procedure), and any predictor's or twin's outputs that depend on the same function or on an accurate model of it. It does *not* update things that merely correlate with the policy — lesion-induced cancer, coincidental calculator colors, background states causally upstream of the agent's computation except via accurate modeling of it.\n\nThe self-reference is harmless by Kleene's second recursion theorem: the model can contain an accurate representation of the very procedure selecting the policy (\"quining\"), without the agent needing to know the value of that representation in advance.\n\n## 4. Worked Examples\n\n### 4.1 Newcomb's problem\n\nA 99%-accurate predictor fills box B with \\$1,000,000 iff she predicted one-boxing. In the P-FDT model, $\\mathrm{Prediction}$ and $\\text{Act}$ are both children of $\\mathrm{pfdt}$. Comparing hypotheticals:\n\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\text{onebox}})$: the predictor almost surely filled box B; expected utility $\\approx 0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000$.\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{pfdt} = \\pi_{\\text{twobox}})$: the box is almost surely empty; expected utility $\\approx 0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000$.\n\nP-FDT one-boxes. No retrocausality is invoked — the agent merely declines to imagine her action changing while the function computing it stays fixed, \"much like we should expect elliptic curves to have different properties if (counterpossibly) Fermat's last theorem were false.\"\n\n### 4.2 Smoking lesion\n\nHere the fatal correlation runs through a lesion, which does not model the agent's decision function. So $\\mathrm{pfdt}$ connects to $\\text{Act}$ but not to $\\text{Cancer}$. Varying the policy does not change cancer probability, and smoking (worth \\$1,000, at probability $p$ of having the lesion that controls her preferences) strictly dominates refraining. P-FDT smokes — one rule, both answers, contra the Gibbard–Harper claim that rationality forces a choice between one-box-and-refrain and two-box-and-smoke.\n\n### 4.3 Transparent Newcomb and Parfit's hitchhiker\n\nBoth boxes transparent, box B visibly full: CDT and EDT two-box, but P-FDT intervenes on $\\mathrm{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G}, \\text{full})$ — the *policy for the full-box observation* — and notes that this very procedure is what the predictor modeled. If it outputs one-box, the box was probably filled; if two-box, probably not. Expected utility favors one-boxing even while staring at a full box. The source notes this is \"precisely equivalent to paying the driver in Parfit's hitchhiker problem,\" and that P-FDT-style agents refuse mechanical blackmail for the same reason: paying corresponds to a world where one is predictably exploitable and hence blackmailed; refusing corresponds to a world where one is probably never blackmailed at all.\n\n### 4.4 Where the policy formulation earns its keep\n\nThe source says the action/policy distinction \"only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell,\" without developing examples — so what follows is my own elaboration, though I believe it is faithful to the footnote's intent.\n\n**Iterated dilemmas.** Suppose you face transparent Newcomb on Monday and Newcomb-with-observation-noise on Tuesday, and the predictor's Monday model of you includes how you'd behave Tuesday. An action-level evaluation at Monday's decision point evaluates \"output one-box *now*\" — it can underweight how the same decision function will handle Tuesday's observations. The policy-level variable $\\mathrm{pfdt}(\\underline{P}, \\underline{G})$ represents the entire input–output profile of the procedure, so the hypothetical in which \"my procedure outputs $\\pi$\" already fixes how I behave at *every* observation, present and future. This is exactly the structure needed for the source's gym example: today's decision and next week's decision are instances of the same policy, so \"she should assume that she will continue going to the gym in the future if she goes today.\"\n\n**Sequential predictions.** Consider a two-round game where a predictor fills a box in round 2 based on her prediction of your round-2 behavior, made after observing your round-1 behavior (perhaps because your round-1 reasoning and round-2 reasoning share code). An action-level agent choosing her round-1 move evaluates it in isolation; a P-FDT agent evaluates the full policy (round-1 move, round-2 move as a function of what she sees), which is the natural unit over which predictors and teammates reason about her. In the source's voting-coalition example — ten agents each paid \\$1,000,000 iff all ten vote — each agent's reasoning is symmetric, and the shared policy structure is what makes voting worthwhile; I take the policy formulation to be the precise home of that argument.\n\n## 5. Dominance, Fairness, and Reflective Stability\n\nEvery method for constructing hypotheticals yields its own dominance relation. Two-boxing \"dominates\" only under CDT counterfactuals — which amounts to holding fixed the behavior of the decision function while varying its output, \"akin to handing us a well-functioning calculator calculating $6288+1048$ and asking us to imagine it outputting $3159$.\" Under FDT-style dominance (holding fixed all subjunctive dependencies), FDT agents never take dominated actions.\n\nThe source's fairness criterion: a problem is *fair* if the agent's beliefs are accurate and the outcome depends only on her behavior in the dilemma at hand. Newcomb predictors punish *two-boxers*, not rational agents as such; dilemmas that punish agents for their decision procedures directly (e.g., \"punish anyone who acts like Fiona\") are unfair, and no theory dominates there. The claim — which the authors themselves flag as unproven — is that P-FDT matches or beats all rivals across fair dilemmas. Relatedly, P-FDT is reflectively stable: facing the blackmail letter, the agent has *no desire* to self-modify into CDT, since a policy that pays blackmail is blackmailed more often. CDT, by contrast, advises agents to abandon it in future Newcomblike problems while continuing to use it mid-dilemma — a temporal inconsistency the authors rightly treat as disqualifying for a normative theory.\n\n## 6. Open Questions and My Interpretive Choices\n\nSeveral points are left open by the source; here is how I read them.\n\n**(a) How is the logical-dependency graph $G$ constructed?** The source is candid: \"Figuring out how to deduce the structure of subjunctive dependencies from observation and experiment is perhaps the largest open problem.\" Pearl gave a story for causal graphs; nothing comparable exists for logical graphs. My operational suggestion: treat $G$ as asserting an edge $\\mathrm{pfdt} \\to Z$ exactly when $Z$'s value is computed *via a process that models or implements* the policy function — simulations, physical implementations, accurate predictors — with imperfect modeling weakening but not eliminating the edge (as with 99%-accurate predictors, where the graph is parameterized by an $\\text{Accurate}$ node). This is a heuristic gloss, not a formal solution.\n\n**(b) Counterpossible reasoning.** Intervening on $\\mathrm{pfdt}$ supposes a false mathematical fact. The source argues this is technical rather than philosophical (humans manage it), and that the needed reasoning is narrow: \"How would this predictor's prediction change if the FDT algorithm had a different output?\" I adopt the source's graphical route precisely because it sidesteps defining a general $\\mathtt{true}$ operator — though footnote 9 notes some hope for an \"evidential version\" via logical conditioning, which currently lacks foundations.\n\n**(c) What is the policy domain?** Footnote 16 leaves $\\Pi$ unspecified. In finite dilemmas, $\\Pi$ is the finite set of functions from the finite observation set to the action set. In sequential problems with memory, observation histories grow; the natural reading is that $\\pi$ maps *state histories* to actions, recovering ordinary sequential-decision structure, with P-FDT then coinciding with action-level FDT in single-shot, single-observation problems — consistent with the footnote's claim that the difference \"only matters in multi-agent dilemmas.\"\n\n**(d) Computational tractability.** Optimizing over policies is exponentially harder than over actions. The source studies idealized agents and expects \"bounded versions... [to] exhibit similar strengths and weaknesses\"; I regard practical approximation (e.g., searching over a restricted policy class) as an open engineering problem, with the risk that approximations reintroduce the very correlation-errors P-FDT avoids.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nPolicy functional decision theory is the authors' preferred formalization of functional decision theory: an expected-utility theory that intervenes on the logical variable representing its *entire policy* — the mapping from observations to actions — rather than on a physical act or a single action-output. The counterfactuals it constructs preserve all subjunctive dependencies (relations mediated by shared computation or accurate modeling) and break merely statistical ones. This single rule one-boxes in ordinary and transparent Newcomb problems, smokes in the smoking lesion problem, pays the hitchhiker, refuses blackmail, stabilizes Death in Damascus, and sustains cooperation and coordination — while remaining reflectively stable and dominance-clean under its own counterfactual semantics. The deepest open problems are the inference of subjunctive-dependence structure from observation, a satisfactory account of counterpossible conditionals, and the general formalization of \"fair\" dilemmas. But as a normative proposal, the core idea is compact: when you weigh your options, do not ask what would happen if your body did something else, nor what it would mean if it did — ask what the world would be like if the policy you are implementing right now were different, and choose accordingly.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":57808,"completion_tokens":4059,"total_tokens":61867,"cost":0.00376566,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":57792,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.00376566,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00173616,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0020295},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}